The CUDA program below has a coordination bug that only appears when you read BOTH snippets together. Snippet A is the device-side wait on a job slot; snippet B is the host-side ring setup and submission.

Snippet A (device):
```cuda
// wait until job slot 'slot' carries a sequence number >= target
__device__ void wait_job(volatile unsigned long long* flags, int slot,
                         unsigned long long target) {
    while (flags[slot] < target) {
        __nanosleep(200);
    }
}
```

Snippet B (host):
```cpp
struct JobRing {
    unsigned long long flags[256];
    Job               jobs[256];
    unsigned long long submitted = 0;
};

void init_ring(JobRing& r) {
    memset(r.flags, 0, sizeof(r.flags));   // start clean
    memset(r.jobs,  0, sizeof(r.jobs));
    r.submitted = 0;
}

unsigned long long submit(JobRing& r, int slot) {
    fill_payload(r, slot);                 // writes r.jobs[slot] first
    r.flags[slot] = ++r.submitted;         // publish sequence AFTER payload
    return r.flags[slot];
}
```

The consumer kernels are launched BEFORE the first submission happens and each waits on its slot with a target value handed in at launch time (target 1 for the first wave of jobs).

Question: find the bug, explain the root cause by combining the semantics of snippet A and snippet B (state the exact sequence of events), give a concrete 3-step interleaving that mis-executes, and name the standard fix. Then say whether waiting with strict equality (==) instead of < would also fix it, and why that answer changes if sequence numbers can wrap around.

Refactor the C++ function below. Keep the observable behaviour EXACTLY identical (same accepted inputs, same results, same error behaviour, same call order of the out-param) while removing the duplication.

```cpp
// Parses two array fields out of a binary metadata record and stores them.
// Returns false if the field is missing or has the wrong element type.
// Field layout: [u32 kind][u32 count][payload...]; kind 1 = u32 elements, kind 2 = u64 elements.
bool parse_fields(const uint8_t* rec, size_t len,
                  std::vector<uint32_t>& a_out, std::vector<uint64_t>& b_out) {
    size_t off = 0;
    auto read_u32 = [&](uint32_t& v) {
        if (off + 4 > len) return false;
        memcpy(&v, rec + off, 4); off += 4; return true;
    };

    uint32_t kind_a, count_a;
    if (!read_u32(kind_a) || !read_u32(count_a)) return false;
    if (kind_a != 1) return false;
    if (off + (size_t)count_a * 4 > len) return false;
    a_out.clear();
    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < count_a; ++i) {
        uint32_t v; memcpy(&v, rec + off, 4); off += 4;
        a_out.push_back(v);
    }

    uint32_t kind_b, count_b;
    if (!read_u32(kind_b) || !read_u32(kind_b)) return false;
    if (kind_b != 2) return false;
    if (off + (size_t)count_b * 8 > len) return false;
    b_out.clear();
    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < count_b; ++i) {
        uint64_t v; memcpy(&v, rec + off, 8); off += 8;
        b_out.push_back(v);
    }
    return true;
}
```

Requirements:
1. State the duplication you remove and how (one generic helper, its signature, what it is parameterised on).
2. Point out every behavioural DIFFERENCE that already exists between the two blocks above (there is at least one genuine bug and one latent portability hazard — find them, do NOT silently fix them beyond what the contract requires, but say what you would change and why).
3. Show the refactored code complete.
